{"id": "live-089", "title": "Kane Creek Development Watch v. Lieutenant Governor of Utah (Echo Canyon Preliminary Municipality Challenge)", "category": "zoning-land-use", "status": "pending", "date": "2026-02-10", "jurisdiction": "Utah Seventh District Court, Grand County", "state": "UT", "amount": "Constitutional challenge; no damages sought", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.sltrib.com/news/2026/02/14/utah-law-shifts-power-elected/", "summary": "Kane Creek Development Watch and Living Rivers sued the Utah Lieutenant Governor and the developer behind Echo Canyon on February 10 in Utah's Seventh District Court, seeking to void the state's 2024 'preliminary municipality' law and the certification it conferred on Echo Canyon, a proposed 176-acre resort development near Moab. The suit alleges SB258 unconstitutionally hands zoning and land-use authority to a developer-controlled governing board without an election or public vote.", "significance": "Utah's preliminary municipality statute is among the most aggressive state-law vehicles yet for letting a developer exercise quasi-governmental zoning authority over its own project, and a ruling on its constitutionality will inform how other states calibrate similar developer-self-governance proposals. Developers and lenders relying on first-of-their-kind statutory entitlement structures should treat certification as provisional, not settled, until the litigation resolves.", "judge": null, "tags": ["zoning", "land use", "utah", "municipal incorporation", "constitutional challenge"]}
